From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Afonchenko <undermove1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:36:02 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Meridian: a Workbench terminal starts without fork on the Mac
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A test build crashed opening a Workbench terminal (27.11 00:10, SIGSEGV):
fork() in the many-threaded browser ran libSystem's atfork handlers in
the child, libxpc's released an XPC object there, and Chromium's
Objective-C zombie tracking (ZombieDealloc) took the child's copy of the
browser down inside fork(), before exec. Present since the terminals
came; rare, but any terminal opening could hit it.

On the Mac the program now starts with posix_spawn, which runs no atfork
handler. posix_spawn can't make the pty the controlling terminal
(TIOCSCTTY: ^C, job control; without it a shell's loop didn't stop on
^C), so it runs the browser's own binary as a trampoline first:
`Meridian --meridian-pty-exec -- <program…>` (helper_modes.cc, before
anything of the browser) takes the pty as its controlling terminal,
resets the signals and execs the program, which keeps that pid. The same
child as before: a new session (POSIX_SPAWN_SETSID), the pty on
stdin/stdout/stderr, no other descriptor (POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT),
no blocked signal, every signal at its default, in the project folder
(where Meridian is when the folder is gone). Linux keeps fork(). A
program that can't be run now says "Couldn't start the terminal."
instead of a terminal that ends with 127.

Unit tests (the test binary is its own trampoline): the child is a
session leader in the pty's foreground group with the pty as /dev/tty,
in its folder, without Meridian's open files; a folder that's gone still
starts the shell; 60 terminals one after another while four threads keep
allocating.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SwxrQ2eLfJ2bv8cCoG2va1
